<P>
Good day to you, and welcome to Unga's guide to blacksmithing for dark races. 
<P>This guide is a compilation of my experience as well as info from other 
guides I have read. The "evil" races of Norath are at a distinct disadvantage in 
the field of Blacksmithing. Two of the most important materials for skilling up 
cheaply, are only found in Freeport or some other "good" city, but I will get to 
that. 
<P>Keep in mind that Verant, in their infinite wisdom, has decided that skill 
increase in smithing is only dependent on Inteligence, and failures are based on 
Wisdom. This poses a problem for troll and ogre warriors, so either bear this in 
mind when creating a Troll or ogre and put some points into both, or buy a lot 
of Int and Wis items if you don't want to spend lots of money. I am an ogre 
shadowknight with an improved int of 87 (base 80), so I have a pretty good learning rate.  The trouble is that I have very low base wisdom (67), and even lower base Charisma (50).  I have upped Wisdom to 71 and Charisma to 78 with various items, so I am beginning to fail less and am able to buy cheaper and sell for more.  <b>Note:</b> Since I originally wrote this, I got rid of my wisdom items and have not noticed a significant failure rate difference, so it may or may not make much difference
<P><B>Starting up (skill 1-21)</B> 
<P>Now, the most basic tools of the trade are Ore, water, a forge and lots of 
platinum. To skill to 21, you have several choices. You can dump skill points 
into smithing to take it up as far as it will let you (some say 15, some say 
20). You can also sharpen rusties to 21 (rusty weapon, sharpening stone), or 
even 27 with more valuable rusties (note, I was unable to sharpen rusty 
halberds). The third option is to make metal bits till skill 21 (two small 
pieces of ore, one flask of water). 
<P>Personally, I put 5 points into smithing, then sharpened weapons till about 
skill 18 and made metal bits to 21 
<P><B>To mule or not to mule (skill 22-41,51)</B> 
<P>This is where it gets interesting. In order to consider to skill up and do it 
cheaply, you will need to make scalers (scaler mold, metal bits, flask of 
water). Don't try finding the molds in an evil city, you won't. You have a 
choice though. You can either mule the molds from Freeport (recommended unless 
you have lots of plat to spend on ore), or you can make metal sheets (two small 
blocks of ore and one flask of water). Metal sheets are a far sight easier than 
Scalers, but they are also a far sight more expensive. The ore is also extremely 
heavy, so Neriak is the best location for making metal sheets since there is a 
forge about 60 paces from The Blind Fish, which is where the ore can be found in 
Neriak. 
<P>Personally, I muled since I did not have the spare plat to spend. I made a 
bard, pumped his char to 110, and had him make all my purchases in Freeport. 
<p><b>NEW:</b> Since very recently, toolboxes have been introduced.  These are made by combining one metal bit, and one flask of water in a forge.  They are a very inexpensive way to skill up to 51.
<P><B>Lanterns (41-68)</B> 
<P>Once scalers or sheets become trivial, it is time to move on to Lanterns. Go 
to the blind fish and fill a few packs with small pieces of ore. Now go to 
Neriak foreign Quarter and make them into metal bits. Once you have only bits in 
the packs, go to the Ogre sisters who sell the pot molds, and buy several 
lantern molds. Next, go to the gobbler in Bites n Pieces and buy a bunch of 
bottles. Now, go to one of the forges and make Lanterns (1 bottle, 1 flask of 
water, 1 lantern casing mold, 1 metal bit. This should skill you up to 68 rather 
quickly. 
<P><B>More Muling, oh joy, well, maybe not (68-101+)</B> 
<P>Skewers, what fun. Again, you can't buy them anywhere. Why not? Because you 
are evil and they are only sold by "good" merchants. Again, the choice is yours. 
You can mule them (kinda boring), or you can start making banded (very 
expensive). I tried muling, but found I was getting nowhere and that my skill 
goes up faster with banded gorgets (1 gorget mold, 1 flask of water, 1 sheet of 
metal). If you do choose to go the banded route, make gorgets like I did. The 
molds are cheaper at 3sp each, and I do not fail too often.  Who knows, you might get lucky and find lots of players that want to buy gorgets, don't count on it though. I've sold 5 or six to players, 
the rest have gone back to the shop.   As I said, this is very expensive.  It took me nearly 400pp to get to master following this guide, but it is really the only choice you have unless you want to skip right to sewing kits.
<P><B>After master (101-135)</B> 
<P>Ahh, sewing kits.  Finally, something that is not so expensive to make.  Still not great, but at least they only cost about 5 gold to make instead of 1.5 plat.  Sewing kits are made by placing one thimble mold, one needle mold, one metal bit, and one water flask in a forge.  I started making them at master since Gorgets become trivial at 105 anyway, and I was flat broke.  I relocated to Oggok, got bound there, and started spending some intimate time with the forge turning one and a half packs worth of ore into metal bits.  The molds can be bought from Murga in the Feerrott.
<p>this should quickly take your skill to 135, from there, you have only one way to skill up.  Keep in mind that this only yields small sewing kits, so don't plan on making any money from them.
<p><b>I just love spending lots of money to skill up (136-175)</b>
<p>Banded is your only option that I know of for skilling past 135.  To the best of my knowledge, mantles will get you to 168 (or so I have read), and cloaks will get you to 175.  You can experiment if you like.
<p><b>Now that I am a Master Smith, what can I make?</b>
<p>Banded is the bread and butter of the trade.  It has slightly less AC than plate, but weighs much less than bronze and thus is often more desirable than the lesser variety of platemail.  Greatsmith's guide has a list of levels when you should be able to make the various peices of banded.  I recommend you wait to mass produce those pieces until at least 10 more skill points than he suggests.  Granted, I am only able to make boots, gorgets, and bracers at present, but I noticed that at 70 skill I failed three out of four times when  making bracers.  Boots at skill 90 were successful 1/5 of the time.  I would assume that this holds true for the other pieces as well.
<p>Keep in mind, I had a wisdom of only 67 when I first attempted boots, so the low success rate is most likely due to that.  I'm an ogre, what can I say.  
<p>For now, Banded is the only armor we can make.  It's not bad, but it's also not the best.  I have a feeling that forged plate is on it's way since they just fixxed the recipe for folded sheets of metal.  There can be only one use for something that expensive (cost me 5 1/2 pp to make when my charisma was 78).  On test, fine plate armor can be made, and there are rumors of special race specific plate that can only be made by certain races.
<p>Weapons can be made as well.  You can get the molds from the ogre sisters just inside the ogre and troll section of Neriak Foreign quarter.  I've never made any forged weapons, but I can see them selling to newbies for a half decent amount.  They are about the same quality as fine steel.
<p>One of the nice things about being a smith is that people need you.  Bakers need you to make pots and pans and the like, tinkers need you for various items, but most importantly, Tailors need you.  You can make studs for studded leather and bonings for reinforced leather.
<p><b>That's all folks</b>
<P>Well, that is it. Not much more to it. Dark races need lots of money to skill 
up in smithing, either that or they need a good mule and a lot of patience. If 
you make a mule, try to pump up the charisma, it helps a great deal. Strength is 
not overly important for the mule as molds are very light, and unless you plan 
to play the character in the future, Charisma is the only stat they need. 
<P>If you have any suggestions or aditions, send e-mail to <A 
href="mailto:ungabunga@onemain.com">Ungabunga</A>. 
<P>As a further thought, if you have a lot of plat, you might want to follow the speedsmithing guide 
found at KC's. You can follow the link below. This is actually the easiest idea 
for Dark races, though not at all money efficient.  <b>NOTE:</b> This will only get you to master smith, after which you can follow my guide.<BR>
